        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The overall goal of this project was to investigate the effects of microgravity on the neural control of motor units within a given muscle and the coordination of a group of muscles controlling a given joint. The main objectives were:</p>
1. To document the effects of microgravity on the control of motor units in the Vastus Medialis (VM), an antigravity muscle of the leg used to a much lesser extent in space than on Earth.<br>
2. To study the effects of microgravity on the First Dorsal Interosseous (FDI), a hand muscle used less in space than on Earth but is nevertheless utilized more than the antigravity muscle of the leg being considered.<br>
3. To study the effects of microgravity on the activation of a group of muscles controlling one joint.<br>
4. To determine the course of recovery from microgravity after being re-exposed to gravity.
<p>
The information gained in this project will assist in understanding why muscles weaken in space and should enable the development of improved countermeasures for maintaining muscle force viability. This is an important issue that is relevant for enhancing the safety of astronauts in space and improving their health during and following extended exposures to microgravity.
<p>
In regard to Earth-based research, the understanding of the neurological modifications that occur in microgravity should provide an accelerated time-scale model for the neurological effect on motor control due to aging. Additionally, the equipment that will be developed for studying the behavior of motor unit motor control can be used by neurologists to assess the degree of severity of upper motor neuron disease such as stroke and Parkinson's disease.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Subjects participated in three pre-flight test sessions at L-45, L-43 and L-41 and six postflight test sessions between R+0, 1 or 2, 3, 5, 7, and 14).  The procedures focused on two areas of neural control of muscle:  1) the regulation of the discharge activities of motor units that make up a given muscle, and 2) the coordination among different muscles that contribute to a given task.  Both properties were studied using intramuscular and surface electromyographic (EMG) techniques.  The procedures investigated two different muscles, the First Dorasl Interosseous (FD), a small hand muscle that controls the index finger, and the Vastus Medialis (VM), a muscle of the thigh that controls knee extension.  These particular muscles were selected becasue they experience different patterns of usage in microgravity.  
<P>
The pre- and postflight protocols are similar.  The subject is seated in a custom-made test chair.  The hand and leg muscles were tested sequentially in the same test session.  Surface EMG sensors were placed on four thigh muscles and one hand muscle.  Maximal voluntary strength measures were taken during knee extension/flexion and index finger abduction.  These measurements were made with custom dynamometers attached to the test chair.  Afterward the indwelling EMG electrode was placed in the thight muscle.  EMG signals from the wires were recorded while the subject execute the two force trajectories:  one at a relatively low force level and the other at a relatively hight force level.  Each test contraction lasted no more than one minute, with ample rest periods provided between the contractiosn.  The same force trajectories and recordings were made from the hand muscle, except the recordings were made from either a surface EMG electrode "array" placed over the FDI muscle or a 25-gauge needle was inserted in the FDI muscle adjacent to a surface EMG array.  
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The investigators composed a final report but there are limited results available because the investigation was discontinued. 
